rs for other languages. This is a genuine architecture decision, not settled board decomposition. Produce an architecture-only decision packet; do not edit product, spec, schema, code, tests, release metadata, or existing decision documents. Resolve: (1) manifest schema shape for a named external Git build repository and immutable revision/lock semantics; (2) repository-root descriptor name, schema version, target selection, monorepo build_root/source_dir rules, and backward compatibility; (3) exact access behavior: syntax-only offline warning, install/audit hard failure when inaccessible, credential ownership, allowed Git transports, local operator substitutions, moved refs, submodules, LFS, symlinks and special files; (4) snapshot and audit equivalence to in-skill source, including compiler-visible root, dependency graph containment, source identity, receipts, marker v2 and protected cache; (5) deterministic build, install, PATH/shim verification and rollback; (6) platform-owned signing policy and the boundary between local signing and release-time notarization; (7) the extension rule for future Rust, Swift, Kotlin/JVM, C/C++, .NET and other closed drivers; (8) exact impact on existing spec, Curator, cocoaskills and interoperability tasks, while Linux remains a separate later native validation. Explicitly reconcile the user proposal that the repository descriptor name the final binary and output location with the accepted protocol rule forbidding package-controlled executable names and output selection. Prefer logical target IDs plus manager-derived command name and private staging path unless evidence supports another closed design. Audit must happen before any compiler execution. Repository access failure must warn during offline/static check when verification cannot be attempted and must fail install before mutation when source cannot be resolved. Credentials and signing identities must never come from the package descriptor. Research authoritative Git, Go build, platform signing/notarization and sandbox constraints as needed. Persist the decision and threat model as a new task-scoped outcome resource, include concrete JSON examples and normative MUST/MUST NOT recommendations, record source links, then route to to-review for an independent architecture review.\\nspawn agent resolution: Agent selection: codex via explicit_override\\nspawn launch composition: degraded_contract_unavailable; contract=agents-infra.child-launch-composition; provider=codex; schema=1; diagnostic=composition_contract_unavailable; bare child launch retained\\nspawn queued: [analyst] solution-architect (codex) (run=RUN-260727-482663, max_parallel=20)\\nspawn run started: [analyst] solution-architect (codex) (run=RUN-260727-482663)\\nArchitecture logbook 2026-07-27 — resolved the external build-repository scope delta without editing project/spec files. Recommendation: freeze schema 6/go-v1; add schema 7, strict build_repositories, locked Git commit with optional tag assertion, curator-build.json schema 1 logical targets, new closed go-repository-v1, receipt v2, marker v3, and claim v3/rc.5. Repository targets cannot select executable names, output paths, argv, environment, credentials, signing, hooks, or plugins; manifest command keys and manager-private staging remain authoritative. Exact external snapshots must be raw-tree validated and independently audited before cache lookup/compiler on real, cache-hit, dry-run, install/repair/audit paths; syntax-only offline checks warn when verification is unavailable, while mutating/coverage-claiming operations fail before mutation. Git submodules, LFS hydration, links/special modes, checkout filters, package credential helpers, and package signing identities are rejected. Local substitutions stay operator-owned in Skillfile.dev v2; release notarization/AuthentiCode stays outside install-time builds; Linux remains separate native validation. Outcome TASK-260720-1nvomm_external-build-repositories-architecture.